#N canvas 100 100 640 600 10;
#X obj 40 40 jet_win;
#X msg 40 100 create;
#X msg 130 100 destroy;
#X msg 250 100 start;
#X msg 340 100 stop;
#X obj 40 150 jet_head 1;
#X obj 40 180 jet_video;
#X obj 300 150 jet_head 0;
#X obj 300 180 jet_perlin 256 256;
#X obj 40 220 jet_shader slitscan.metal;
#X obj 40 250 jet_rectangle 4.2 3.2;
#X msg 250 220 maxdelay 10;
#X msg 340 220 maxdelay 30;
#X msg 250 260 freeze 1;
#X msg 340 260 freeze 0;
#X text 40 300 jet_shader slitscan.metal - the slit-scan as a shader file instead of a dedicated object. Per-PIXEL delay \, picked by the red channel of a second "delay map" texture (black = now \, white = oldest). The map comes from the other chain: jet_perlin -> [jet_share delaymap] \, and the shader's "@input map delaymap" line picks it up by name (the symbol inlet of the shader would take another name). The history ring buffer comes from "@history 30" in the file \; maxdelay (0..30 frames) is a normal parameter. Open shaders/slitscan.metal in a text editor and change something while this runs - the picture follows when you save. Try a gradient image instead of jet_perlin for a classic scanline slit-scan look.;
#X obj 300 210 jet_share delaymap;
#X connect 1 0 0 0;
#X connect 2 0 0 0;
#X connect 3 0 6 0;
#X connect 4 0 6 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 9 0;
#X connect 7 0 8 0;
#X connect 9 0 10 0;
#X connect 11 0 9 0;
#X connect 12 0 9 0;
#X connect 13 0 9 0;
#X connect 14 0 9 0;
#X connect 8 0 16 0;
